      <div className="space-y-5">
        <p>Vocabulary is the invisible scaffold of academic performance. Reading comprehension depends on it - you cannot understand what you cannot decode. Essay quality depends on it - you cannot write with precision using only the words readily available to you. Academic reasoning depends on it - the concepts you can think with are partly determined by the concepts your vocabulary makes available. Science, history, mathematics, literature, and every other academic discipline has domain-specific vocabulary that separates students who genuinely understand a subject from students who are processing it at the surface level. The student with the strongest vocabulary across all these domains is rarely the best-performing student in every individual subject - but they are almost never the one who struggles.</p>

        <h3 className="text-xl font-black mt-8 mb-3">Understanding Why Academic Vocabulary Is Different From General Vocabulary</h3>
        <p>Academic vocabulary operates at three distinct tiers, and understanding this structure is essential for prioritising your vocabulary development effectively. Tier 1 words are common everyday words - run, house, happy, quickly - that most students learn through normal life experience without deliberate instruction. Tier 3 words are the highly specialised technical terms of specific disciplines - photosynthesis, Keynesian, isosceles, synecdoche - that are taught explicitly within their subject areas because they rarely appear outside them.</p>
        <p>Tier 2 words are where most academic vocabulary weakness lives, and where vocabulary development produces the greatest cross-subject returns. These are high-frequency academic words that appear across multiple subjects and in academic writing generally - words like analyse, evaluate, synthesise, interpret, formulate, justify, infer, derive, corroborate, and contextualize. A student who does not fully understand what "analyse" means will struggle with analysis tasks across English, history, science, and mathematics. A student who does not understand "evaluate" cannot properly respond to evaluation questions in any subject. Weakness in Tier 2 academic vocabulary is a lever that depresses performance across the entire academic experience simultaneously.</p>

        <h3 className="text-xl font-black mt-8 mb-3">The Tier 2 Words Every Student Needs to Own</h3>
        <p>These cross-subject academic words appear in exam questions, textbook explanations, essay prompts, and teacher instructions across all disciplines. Knowing them precisely - not approximately but precisely - is one of the highest-return vocabulary investments a student can make:</p>
        <ul className="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 pl-2">
          {["Analyse - examine in detail, identifying components, their relationships, and their significance. Not summarise. Not describe. Examine and interpret.", "Evaluate - assess the value, significance, quality, or validity of something, usually by considering multiple perspectives or criteria. Requires judgment, not just description.", "Synthesise - combine information from multiple sources or ideas to form a new, integrated whole. The emphasis is on integration and the creation of something coherent from diverse inputs.", "Hypothesise - propose a possible explanation or prediction to be tested or supported with evidence. Involves both creativity and logical structure.", "Contextualise - place information or ideas within their broader historical, social, cultural, or intellectual circumstances to enable fuller understanding.", "Derive - obtain something through a specific process or by working from established premises. In mathematics: calculate. In argument: conclude logically from premises.", "Elucidate - make something difficult or complex clearer and easier to understand by explaining it in greater detail or with examples.", "Juxtapose - place two things side by side specifically for the purpose of revealing contrast or highlighting differences between them."].map(w => <li key={w}>{w}</li>)}
        </ul>

        <h3 className="text-xl font-black mt-8 mb-3">The Subject-Specific Vocabulary Journal System</h3>
        <p>Every student should maintain a vocabulary journal - organised by subject - that records every unfamiliar academic term encountered during study. The journal entry for each word should include: the word and its definition in your own words (not copied from the textbook); the sentence or context in which you encountered it; a sentence you write yourself using the word in the context of the subject where you learned it; and the date of first encounter so you can track when it needs review.</p>
        <p>The subject organisation is important because it creates domain-specific semantic clusters - the way the word connects to other words in that subject's vocabulary - which makes each word easier to retain within its disciplinary context and easier to retrieve when you need it for assignments, discussions, and exams in that subject. A vocabulary journal maintained consistently through a school year produces a searchable, comprehensive record of academic vocabulary acquisition that becomes genuinely useful for revision as exams approach.</p>
        <p>Review your vocabulary journal weekly - just the words from the previous seven days - and monthly - a broader review of all words added in the past thirty days. The weekly review is spaced repetition in practice: reviewing at approximately the right interval to refresh the memory trace before significant forgetting occurs. The monthly review catches words that are beginning to fade and returns them to active rehearsal before they disappear.</p>

        <h3 className="text-xl font-black mt-8 mb-3">Root Words as Learning Multipliers</h3>
        <p>Learning Latin and Greek roots is one of the highest-leverage vocabulary investments available to any student, because each root unlocks multiple words simultaneously rather than producing one word per unit of study. A student who learns the Latin root "PORT" (to carry) simultaneously gains insight into transport, import, export, portable, portfolio, deportment, rapport, and a dozen other words without having to study each one individually. A student who learns the Greek root "GRAPH" (to write) gains access to photograph, biography, autograph, graphic, paragraph, typography, calligraphy, choreography, and many more. Root knowledge is a force multiplier for vocabulary acquisition - it turns the study of one linguistic element into the understanding of many words.</p>
        <p>The most productive roots for academic vocabulary development:</p>
        <ul className="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 pl-2">
          <li><strong>CRED</strong> (believe) → credible, credit, incredible, discredit, credential, credence, creed</li>
          <li><strong>DICT</strong> (say/proclaim) → predict, dictionary, contradict, verdict, dictate, diction, edict</li>
          <li><strong>SPEC/SPECT</strong> (look/see) → inspect, retrospect, spectator, perspective, spectrum, circumspect</li>
          <li><strong>SCRIB/SCRIPT</strong> (write) → describe, prescription, manuscript, subscribe, transcribe, inscribe</li>
          <li><strong>MIT/MISS</strong> (send) → submit, transmit, mission, admit, emit, permission, commission</li>
          <li><strong>VID/VIS</strong> (see) → visible, video, vision, supervise, revision, evident, provide</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 className="text-xl font-black mt-8 mb-3">Using New Words in Your Written Assignments</h3>
        <p>The fastest path from passive to active vocabulary for students is to use every new academic word you learn in your next written assignment - once, correctly, in context. Not forced or awkward insertion - genuine use in a sentence where that word is actually the right choice. Teachers notice when student writing includes vocabulary that goes beyond the minimum required. More importantly, using a word correctly in a real academic context cements it in your active vocabulary in a way that no amount of definition reviewing can achieve.</p>
        <p>The principle of "use it or lose it" is particularly strong in academic vocabulary. Words you study in preparation for an exam but never use in actual writing tend to fade quickly after the exam, because they are stored in memory as items you learned for a test rather than as tools you actually deploy. Words you have used in your own writing stick, because the writing event created a personal production memory that is far more durable than a study event. An essay written with three carefully chosen vocabulary words that you just learned is more memorable for you and for your teacher than one that plays it entirely safe with familiar language.</p>

        <h3 className="text-xl font-black mt-8 mb-3">Reading As the Foundation of Academic Vocabulary</h3>
        <p>All the deliberate vocabulary strategies described here work best on a foundation of consistent, wide reading. Reading is the primary mechanism through which vocabulary grows naturally and sustainably - the exposure to words in rich contexts, serving genuine communicative purposes, carrying the weight of real arguments and stories, is the most organic and the most durable form of vocabulary input. Students who read widely across subjects and genres develop vocabulary that is not just broader but deeper - they understand words in context rather than in isolation, which is exactly what academic work demands.</p>
        <p>The recommendation is practical: read at least one non-required piece of writing per day, in a domain or genre you find genuinely interesting. Not for vocabulary study. For genuine engagement. The vocabulary will come along as a passenger, and the quality of its encoding - embedded in something you actually cared about reading - will be higher than anything a deliberate study session can reliably produce. The student with the best vocabulary doesn't always get the best marks. But they are almost never the student who struggles.</p>
      </div>
